# PDF and Design Implementation

When the user provides a PDF, Figma export, screenshot, or mockup, treat it as the visual source of truth for designed parts, and as the style guide for undesigned parts.

## Input Handling

- Prefer page images from the PDF. If only a PDF is provided, ask for or generate clear page renders/screenshots of each relevant page when needed for fidelity.
- Work page by page and section by section. Do not invent conflicts with explicit design frames.
- If multiple pages exist, extract a shared design system before implementing one-off styles.
- When the user attaches images of the PDF pages, inspect them carefully for spacing, type, color, alignment, and component structure.

## Phase 1 — Extract the Design System from the PDF

Before coding, identify and reuse:

- color palette (brand, text, surfaces, borders, accents, states)
- typography scale (families, sizes, weights, line-heights, letter-spacing)
- spacing rhythm and section paddings
- radius, borders, shadows, and elevation
- button/link/input styles and states if visible
- grid, container width, and alignment rules
- icon style and image treatment
- motion cues only if clearly implied

Turn repeated values into tokens/utilities/components when the project supports that.

## Phase 2 — Pixel-Faithful Implementation for Designed Sections

For sections that appear in the PDF/design:

- Implement as close as practical to the design: layout, spacing, typography, color, hierarchy, and component structure.
- Match desktop composition first when the PDF is desktop-first, then adapt responsively without destroying the design intent.
- Preserve visual hierarchy and relative proportions from the design.
- Do not “simplify away” important brand details, decorative structure, or section rhythm unless technically necessary.
- If exact fonts/assets are missing, choose the closest available match and note the substitution.
- Static designs usually show one state. Still implement production states: hover, focus-visible, active, disabled, loading, error, empty, and mobile equivalents.
- Prefer reusable components over page-only duplication when the same pattern repeats in the PDF.

Fidelity checklist for each designed section:

- container width and alignment
- section padding and gaps
- heading/body size and weight
- colors and contrast
- media placement and cropping intent
- CTA style and placement
- card/list/grid structure
- divider/line treatments
- icon sizes and optical alignment

## Phase 3 — Creative Fill for Missing Sections

For sections, pages, or states that are requested but not shown in the PDF:

- Do not paste a generic unrelated UI style.
- Extend the PDF’s design language: same tokens, type scale, spacing rhythm, radius, button language, and density.
- Invent layout creatively, but keep brand continuity obvious.
- Prefer compositions that feel like a natural continuation of the designed pages.
- Include complete UX for missing parts: responsive layout, interaction states, empty/loading/error if data-driven.
- If content is unspecified, use realistic placeholder content that matches the product tone.
- When creativity is required, briefly explain the design decisions and how they derive from the PDF system.

Creative-fill priority order:

1. reuse patterns already visible in the PDF
2. adapt nearby sections of the same page
3. invent a new section that still feels native to the same brand system

## Conflict Resolution

- Explicit PDF design wins over generic taste for designed sections.
- Accessibility, security, and responsive usability win over blind pixel-copy when a conflict exists.
- If a PDF detail is impossible or harmful on web (unreadable type size, impossible hover-only essential action, broken contrast), implement the closest accessible equivalent and note the adaptation.
- If the PDF and the existing codebase design system conflict, ask which source of truth should win; if the user said “follow the PDF”, follow the PDF for the requested surface while still integrating cleanly into the codebase.

## Delivery Expectations for Design Tasks

A design implementation is complete only when:

- designed sections closely match the PDF/mockup
- missing sections feel on-brand and intentionally designed
- responsive behavior is handled
- interactive and edge states are implemented
- tokens/components are reused where appropriate
- accessibility and production quality are not sacrificed for visuals
